The search for ""iOS Safari" autoplay "muted" "playsinline" without user gesture" returned the following summary:
To get an HTML5 `<video>` to autoplay on **iOS Safari** without requiring a user gesture, your video element **must** satisfy specific strict constraints enforced by WebKit[1][2]. 

If any of these requirements are missing, iOS Safari will block the autoplay and might throw a DOMException (`NotAllowedError`) when calling `.play()`.

### 1. The Correct HTML Markup
Your video tag must include **all three** of these attributes[3][4]:
*   `autoplay`: Tells the browser to start playing immediately[1].
*   `muted`: Essential. iOS strictly blocks autoplay if there is an audio track unless it is explicitly muted[1]. *(Note: Setting `muted` via JavaScript after the fact can sometimes fail; it needs to be in the initial HTML or properties).*
*   `playsinline` (or `playsInline` in frameworks like React): **Crucial for iOS.** Without this, Safari will try to force the video to open/play in a native fullscreen player, which generally blocks automated/inline autoplay[5].

```html
<video autoplay muted playsinline loop>
  <source src="your-video.mp4" type="video/mp4">
  Your browser does not support the video tag.
</video>
```

---

### 2. JavaScript Implementation (If using `.play()`)
If you are triggering playback via JavaScript rather than purely through the `autoplay` HTML attribute, you must ensure the video is already `muted` and call `.play()` properly handling its returned Promise[1][6]:

```javascript
const videoElement = document.querySelector('video');

// Ensure properties are explicitly set in JS as well
videoElement.muted = true;
videoElement.playsInline = true;

const playPromise = videoElement.play();

if (playPromise !== undefined) {
  playPromise.then(_ => {
    // Autoplay started successfully!
  }).catch(error => {
    // Autoplay was prevented. 
    // This happens if low-power mode is on, browser settings block it, 
    // or attributes (like muted/playsinline) were missing.
    console.log("Autoplay blocked: ", error);
  });
}
```

---

### 3. Additional Conditions & Edge Cases on iOS Safari
Even with `autoplay`, `muted`, and `playsinline`, iOS Safari can still block playback under these conditions:

1. **Visibility in Viewport:** The video element **must be visible on-screen** (within the current viewport) when it loads[1][2]. If it is hidden via CSS (`display: none`), way down the page, or inside an unselected tab/carousel, it will not play until it scrolls into view[2].
2. **Low Power Mode:** If the iOS device is in **Low Power Mode**, Safari frequently overrides developer autoplay policies and blocks all unprompted video/media playback entirely to save battery. Always provide a fallback UI (like a play button or a static poster image)[6].
3. **User Engagement / Power Preferences:** If a user has manually changed global Safari settings to disable autoplay, your code will be blocked.

